WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.590
In 1953 came Watson and Crick,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.190
who,

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.260
with the help from colleagues Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins,

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.150
discovered how the six billion base pairs that make up our genetic material are stored in a 

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:12.650
cell;

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.490
that DNA was actually a helix made of two strands.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.510
However,

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:19.550
the question lingered:

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.350
How does DNA replicate?

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.460
There were a few theories,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.500
three, actually.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.770
One of them was conservative replication, where a DNA molecule would get copied 

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.980
and make a second new red DNA molecule.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.770
The second was dispersive,

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.010
where the DNA molecule would be cut at various parts,

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.760
each of which would get copied here in red and reattached to produce two DNA 

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:44.450
molecules.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.580
And the third was semi-conservative,

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.100
where two DNA strands would separate,

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:55.060
and each one would serve as a template to copy a second red strand, thus producing 

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:56.350
two DNA molecules.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.050
But which one was it?

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.010
Many scientists did not believe that two DNA strands could separate because of how strong a DNA molecule 

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:04.350
is,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.250
so they were skeptical of the semi-conservative model.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.850
The only way to solve this was to create an experiment,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.450
to tell the new DNA strand from the old one.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.890
So two scientists,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.200
Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl,

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:23.820
had an idea to design an experiment that would be known as the most beautiful experiment in 

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:24.500
biology.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.890
Their findings are published in their article titled, "The Replication of DNA in E.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.400
Coli,"

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.300
published in May 1958 in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.620
These scientists use bacteria to answer the question of DNA replication,

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.150
since it could be easily grown in the laboratory.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.680
They grew the cells in the presence of a specific type of nitrogen,

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:47.900
which is found in DNA,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.670
that would make all of the DNA very heavy.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.240
They used centrifugation,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.050
which can separate things according to their weight.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:57.390
Initially,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.350
all of the DNA in the cell was heavy and was at the bottom of the tube since it was grown in heavy 

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:02.980
nitrogen.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.450
Then they started growing these cells in the presence of light nitrogen,

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.650
so all of the DNA made in subsequent cell divisions would be lighter.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.810
After one cell division,

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.550
the DNA was half as heavy,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.770
so half of the DNA molecule contained heavy nitrogen,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.640
and the other half didn't. This is not in line with the conservative DNA replication 

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.050
model,

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.350
which would predict that one molecule be all light, and the other all heavy.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:29.550
However,

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.250
this is in line with the semi-conservative or the dispersive model. 

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.810
After two cell divisions,

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.510
the DNA molecule was now either half heavy and half light, or entirely 

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.050
light.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.540
So this is now not in line with the disperse of model,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.190
which would predict that the DNA after two cell divisions would contain a mixture of heavy and light 

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:49.750
DNA.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.480
Instead,

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.180
this experiment agrees with the semi-conservative DNA replication model.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.250
Every cell gets one old DNA strand and one new one. 

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.240
By discovering that two strands of DNA can separate and provide the template for the production of two 

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.150
new strands,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.370
Meselson and Stahl revolutionized genetics.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.600
Scientists were very skeptical that this was even possible,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:18.550
but Meselson and Stahl proved it with a beautifully designed experiment.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.810
They also went on to show that heating DNA can cause the DNA strands to separate.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:29.230
This simple concept is the basis for an important technique we frequently use in the laboratory called 

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:30.660
polymerase chain reaction,

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.370
which is used in genetic testing, forensics and paternity tests. 

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:41.670
Meselson and Stahl identified the fundamental process by which our DNA gets passed on from 

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:42.450
cell to cell.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.530
This is the basis for inheritance and an essential part of our development,

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:51.490
from a single cell to a complete organism that maintains the same DNA sequence in all of its 

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:52.050
cells.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:56.650
This exciting finding opened up many new doors and research avenues.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.300
Because DNA is the command center of the cell,

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.550
we could now study how DNA gives orders for the cell to follow.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.700
What controls the ability of DNA to make protein?

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.170
How does one cell know it's a heart cell and another cell know it's a liver cell if they all 

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.880
have the same DNA? Now, DNA wasn't just a static double helix;

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.990
it was a molecule that was animated.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.520
It could make more of itself.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.050
It could instruct a cell to behave a certain way.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.640
All of the secrets of biology were ready to be answered. Today, we understand many of these 

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:28.360
questions.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:31.590
We know that the process of DNA replication is very complex,

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:34.250
with many proteins coming together to make this happen,

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:39.450
as well as proofreading and repair mechanisms to make sure that DNA is properly copied.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:45.260
It's amazing now to look back at this very fundamental finding, that DNA replicates in the 

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:50.120
semi-conservative manner, and see how it has revolutionized biology and our appreciation for 

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:50.650
DNA.

